1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,610
Down 28% from its high. Bargain, right?

2
00:00:03,611 --> 00:00:07,999
Weeks earlier it was the world's most valuable company.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,203
Every 20% lower, our buyer added another $10,000.

4
00:00:12,204 --> 00:00:15,999
Five buys. $50,000 in by March 2001.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,777
By October 2002 it was down 88% from the top.

6
00:00:20,778 --> 00:00:23,999
The $50,000 was worth about $17,500.

7
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,787
The clues were there. Every bounce topped out lower.

8
00:00:28,788 --> 00:00:33,999
And every new buy went into a trade already losing.

9
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,999
"Averaging down lowers my cost."
Buying more as it falls is called averaging down.

10
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,999
It lowers your average price. And grows the bet.

11
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:47,406
One buy and a stop 8% below it? Out for $800.

12
00:00:47,407 --> 00:00:51,999
Never add to a loser. Livermore and O'Neil agree.

13
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:57,949
Lore 009. Next: Lore 010, Legend 02: William O'Neil
